$ mp search --help
Search emails with one grammar that every backend speaks.

Fields (combine freely, implicit AND between them):
  from:  to:  cc:  subject:  body:  filename:   match a field
  has:attachment                                 only mail with an attachment
  before:YYYY-MM-DD  after:YYYY-MM-DD             a date range (since: aliases after:)
  in:MAILBOX                                      scope to one mailbox
  message-id:<id>                                 exact Message-ID lookup

Operators:
  "a phrase"        a quoted phrase is one term
  a OR b            either term
  (a OR b)          a parenthesised OR group, AND-ed with the rest

Examples:
  mp search 'from:boss@corp.com (invoice OR receipt) has:attachment'
  mp search --from boss@corp.com --has-attachment 'invoice OR receipt'
  mp search 'subject:"quarterly report" after:2026-01-01 before:2026-07-01'
  mp search --local 'from:ada ledger'

Backend honesty: on Gmail and Exchange every term runs server-side. On plain
IMAP has:attachment has no server key, so it is answered from the local store
(synced mail only): the run warns on stderr, the result header on stdout says
"local store only: has:attachment", and --json sets coverage to "local" with
the term in local_terms. filename: is Gmail/Exchange/--local only.

The daemon runs a server search, the same one the TUI and the desktop app run:
each mailbox is asked for its newest --limit matches, those are merged newest
first and cut to --limit, and only the survivors are downloaded. A mailbox the
server refuses does not stop the others; a "Search in <mailbox> failed" line
on stderr follows the results.

Usage: mp search [OPTIONS] [QUERY]

Arguments:
  [QUERY]
          Search query. One grammar for every backend: fields from: to: cc: subject: body: filename:, the flag has:attachment, dates before:YYYY-MM-DD / after:YYYY-MM-DD (since: aliases after:), quoted "phrases", OR and (a OR b) groups, plus in: and message-id:. Combine with the flags below; they build the same query
          
          [default: ]

Options:
      --mailbox <MAILBOX>
          Mailbox to search: by role, label or server name, any folder the server lists (default: every folder the account syncs, and on Exchange every folder; --local: every mailbox the store holds)

      --from <FROM>
          Match the sender (from:)

      --to <TO>
          Match a recipient (to:)

      --cc <CC>
          Match a Cc recipient (cc:)

      --subject <SUBJECT>
          Match the subject (subject:)

      --body <BODY>
          Match the body (body:)

      --filename <FILENAME>
          Match an attachment filename (Gmail/Exchange/--local only)

      --has-attachment
          Only mail carrying an attachment (has:attachment)

      --after <AFTER>
          On or after this date, YYYY-MM-DD (after:)

      --before <BEFORE>
          Strictly before this date, YYYY-MM-DD (before:)

  -n, --limit <LIMIT>
          Max results across every searched mailbox, the newest winning wherever they sit; a server search cut by it ends with a "raise -n" line. Exchange stops a search at 1000 results per mailbox, which a line says instead
          
          [default: 20]

      --full
          Show full body instead of preview

      --local
          Search the local store's full-text index instead of the server (offline, ranked, covers every synced mailbox at once)

      --json
          Emit one JSON object {account, query, source: local|server, coverage: server|local, local_terms: [...], truncated: bool, messages: [...]} on stdout, messages as in `mp list-messages --json` plus body (--full) or preview (server hit); coverage is local when the store answered any term, which local_terms names (empty under --local, where the store answered the whole query); truncated is true when more matched than --limit let through; warnings stay on stderr

  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>
          Account to use

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ mp config --help
Manage configuration

Usage: mp config [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  init           Interactive setup wizard
  show           Show current configuration
  set-password   Store a password in the active secrets backend (for the first account unless `-A` names one)
  reset-secrets  Wipe the encrypted secrets file (and Graph token caches) and re-prompt for credentials. Use this after a Time Machine restore to a new machine, or whenever the secrets file can no longer be decrypted
  add-account    Add a new account to the existing config
  oauth2-login   Sign a Microsoft 365 (Graph) account in with the OAuth2 device code flow and cache its token (for the first Graph account unless `-A` names one)
  path           Print config file path
  help           Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -A, --account <ACCOUNT>  Account to use
  -h, --help               Print help

